o  our former tenant at our space I quality  eats it was a gin mill and so that has  strawberries a good summer yes delicious  right mm-hmm and there's cucumber cubes  in there it's delicious so you started  we said we said coming in that you kind  of studied at the school of Julia Child  there's a whole class of chefs it seems  that she essentially graduated through  her TV show deaf  you know I think that was sort of this  was like pre Food Network and so PBS was  sort of like your only source of all  this information about food and Julia  was like was like the Queen and I would  what attracted you to it I think it  started early in life I was I come from  a large family six kids so my my mom and  my dad by the time they got to me I was  a youngest of six they really were  pretty easygoing about being in the  kitchen and I was sort of standing on  top of a chair at the stove and I always  felt comfortable so it seemed like a  natural transition for me to sort of do  it as a as a profession yet you started  off pre-law in college when did you know  that was that was the biggest mistake I  could never actually it's the biggest  mistake I avoided so yeah I was I was  studying rhetoric at Cal Berkeley and  and it was a pre-law major I don't  really know what I was doing but I  quickly found out that we've all had  those moments yeah I was I was  outclassed by my my my fellow classmates  so and my interest turned towards food I  was working in a grocery store at that  time and we had chefs coming through and  I was talking to them about produce and  and they were talking to me about what  they would do with it and I was just  really intrigued so it I was just sort  of drawn to it yeah some minimum-wage  jobs you were working in kitchens but  what was sort of that first real get  where you were able to really focus on  the food what was that job for you well  I think when I moved to New York and I  got into the kitchen of lespinasse and  this was in the 90s and chef gray Kunz  was the chef and it was just it was so  far beyond anything I had experienced to  that point his mixture of French  technique and all these like really  exotic Asian ingredients and Indian  spices and it was it was that was like  opened up for me what cooking could be  you preside you were telling us now over  eight restaurants right soon to be a  nine soon to be nine yeah we're opening  another one in Manhattan  early July I'm always amazed at how you  keep something like that going it's it's  a lot of work but really you know it's  like anything it's it's building a good  team again we have an amazing team we  have people that have been with us for  in our company for like close to 40  years our company goes back way way back  to 1977 with Smith Malinsky mm-hmm and  so so you know we have people that have  been with us for that entire time and  those people are really what keeps us  going it is amazing just the work that  you're doing and also the idea of taking  these larger restaurants and keeping  them  I guess feeling more casual and  comfortable how do you do that um well  it's again it's your employees you know  you have to be able to make people feel  like they're a true guest like they're  in your home they're coming to your  house oh and that's how we treat them  you know everybody matters you know and  and if you can get that across to your  employees and they can get that across  to the guests then you're going to have  a great restaurant well it is the dish  so we want you to sign and as you're  signing let me ask you if you could have  a meal with anyone past or present who  would it be I would have to say my mom I  don't really get together enough with  her and I think she'd be really proud of  and she doesn't really get out to the  East Coast so she'd be really proud to  see you know what we've accomplished out  here mama is always a good choice it is  and it is all delicious chef chef Craig  Kotetsu thank you very much we  appreciate you be